import type { StateNote } from './state-notes';
export const NOTES_B: Record<string, StateNote> = {
  MO: {
    intro: `Estimate what a Missouri county pays for your days in the jury box, test your excuse against RSMo § 494.430 and see how the free postponement works.`,
    resume: `Missouri sets a low statutory floor for jurors: $6 a day plus round-trip mileage at the state-employee rate, under RSMo § 494.455. Counties may add to it, so the $6 is a minimum; a county that adds $6 reaches at least $18 in total, with $6 reimbursed by the state, and a county can instead choose $50 a day from the third day with nothing for the first two. No statute obliges an employer to keep paying wages, but RSMo § 494.460 bars an employer from requiring or even requesting that you use vacation, sick or personal leave. People 75 or older are excused if they apply to the court in time, and anyone who sat on a state or federal jury in the preceding two years can be excused on timely application. We found no term length or one-day-one-trial rule in the Missouri text we read. A willful no-show is civil contempt under RSMo § 494.450: up to $500 and/or community service, with no jail term in the statute.`,
    body: `<h2>Missouri's two county pay models</h2><p>Because pay in Missouri is a floor rather than a fixed figure, two neighbors summoned in different counties can be paid very differently. A county either builds on the $6 daily base (adding at least $6 brings the total to at least $18, and the state reimburses $6 of it) or leaves the daily model and pays $50 a day starting on the third day, with days one and two unpaid. For a short stint the first model tends to pay more; on a long trial the $50 option pulls ahead from day three. Your summons or the county jury office tells you which one applies.</p><h2>One Missouri postponement without giving a reason</h2><p>Every Missouri juror gets one postponement as of right: contact the board of jury commissioners in person, by phone, by email or in writing, and the new date falls within 6 months (12 months for full-time students). A second one needs an unforeseeable extreme emergency. Separately, a nursing mother with a physician statement can be excused, as can a health care provider whose absence would harm patients or an employee of a religious institution whose obligations forbid service. Hardship requests go to a judge and need a ruling by your appearance date, so file early.</p>`,
  },
  MT: {
    intro: `Work out Montana's $12 panel fee and the extra $13 for seated jurors, then check whether age, breastfeeding or caregiving lets you ask to be excused.`,
    resume: `Montana pays jurors in two tiers under Mont. Code Ann. § 3-15-201. Everyone on the panel receives $12 a day, and a juror actually selected for a case gets an additional $13 a day, for $25 in total, while serving; mileage is paid each way between home and court under MCA 2-18-503. A juror excused at their own request on the first day forfeits both the per diem and mileage. We located no statute on wages during service, so whether an employer must pay is not set in the text we read; a county clerk page states that the job is protected and that you should give your employer reasonable notice. Anyone 75 or older who asks must be excused (§ 3-15-313(5)). Counties draw jurors for a jury term, such as Lewis and Clark County's one-year term from September to August. Excuse requests go to the jury commissioner by affidavit. Willfully skipping a summons can lead to attachment and a fine of up to $50 under § 3-15-321, with no jail term named.`,
    body: `<h2>The Montana panel fee versus the seated juror rate</h2><p>Most people who answer a Montana summons sit in the panel and are paid $12 for that day. The figure rises only if you are chosen for the jury: the statute adds $13 a day, so a sworn juror receives $25 daily while the trial lasts. One detail matters on day one. If you ask to be excused that first day and the court agrees, you lose the per diem and the mileage for it, so a request made earlier, by affidavit, is the cleaner route.</p><h2>Blocking dates and asking for an excuse in Montana</h2><p>Montana counties work with a jury term, and you can be drawn for any trial inside it. Before you are drawn for a specific trial, you can ask the Clerk of District Court to mark you unavailable for set dates, for example a planned vacation or a doctor appointment. For a full excuse, send the jury commissioner an affidavit stating the facts; the court decides. Listed grounds include undue hardship for you, a dependent or the public you serve, breastfeeding, personal care of a sick, aged or special-needs dependent, and age 75 or older. A person with a chronic incapacity can ask for permanent exclusion with a physician's certificate. After serving on a trial, you are generally not called again for about two months.</p>`,
  },
  NE: {
    intro: `See how Nebraska's $35 juror fee interacts with your paycheck, since employers there may only deduct that fee, and check the written exemption for people 70 and older.`,
    resume: `In Nebraska, each day of required service pays $35 plus mileage, and the county clerk mails the check 2 to 4 weeks later (Neb. Rev. Stat. § 25-1640, as cited on court forms). What sets the state apart is the employer rule in § 25-1674: an employer may not dock your pay for jury service and may reduce wages only by the $35 daily fee, excluding expense reimbursements, and this covers all employers. Shift workers must be excused from shift work on request, and you owe your employer reasonable notice of the summons; an employer who breaks the rule commits a Class IV misdemeanor. People 70 or older can be exempted by written request. You need not serve if, in the past 5 years, you sat more than 4 weeks as a petit juror or on more than one grand jury. Ignoring a summons can lead to contempt of court; no fine or jail figure was stated in what we read, and the statute site itself could not be reached.`,
    body: `<h2>Nebraska employers keep paying, minus the fee</h2><p>Few states go as far as Nebraska on wages. Under § 25-1674, your employer cannot cut your pay because you were on a jury; the only permitted reduction is the $35 per day the court pays you, and mileage or other expense reimbursements cannot be subtracted. The rule applies to every employer, whatever its size. If you work shifts, ask to be released from your shift work; the employer must grant it. Give reasonable notice as soon as the summons arrives so the arrangement is clear.</p><h2>Postponing jury service in Nebraska</h2><p>To move your date, file form DC 8:4, the Application for Postponement of Jury Service, with the judge, showing undue hardship, extreme inconvenience or public necessity; a postponement is granted for a limited period. For an exemption, write to the clerk of the court that summoned you right away and attach proof: a physician's statement for a physical or mental disability, a physician's certificate for a nursing mother, documentation for active military duty, or details of prior service within the 5-year window. People 70 or older only need a written request. A judge reviews each one, so keep a copy and follow the clerk's instructions until you hear back.</p>`,
  },
  NV: {
    intro: `Find out when Nevada's $65 daily juror pay actually starts, what your employer cannot ask of you before reporting, and which age-based excuses NRS 6.020 allows.`,
    days: 5,
    resume: `Nevada's juror fee under NRS 6.150 is $65 a day, but it begins on the third day of jury selection, or on the day you are sworn in if that comes sooner; the first two days of selection are unpaid for anyone not sworn. NRS 6.190 does not make employers pay wages, yet it forbids forcing you to use sick leave or vacation and limits work around your service, and you should give the employer notice at least 3 days before reporting. People 70 or over may ask by affidavit to be excused from all service, as may those 65 or older living 65 miles or more from the court (NRS 6.020). In Clark County, service ends the first day if you are not assigned to a panel or selected, sworn jurors sit for the trial (3 to 5 days on average), and you are not called again for at least 18 months. Without good cause, a no-show faces contempt and a fine of up to $500 under NRS 6.040.`,
    body: `<h2>Why the first Nevada days may pay nothing</h2><p>The Nevada fee schedule rewards the jurors who end up hearing a case. During selection, days one and two carry no fee; pay of $65 a day starts on day three, or immediately once you are sworn as a juror. In Clark County, a juror not assigned to a panel or selected on the first day is finished that day, and so earns no fee. Mileage at 36.5 cents per mile applies only when your home is at least 30 miles from the courthouse, one way. Set the calculator to five days to see how the total builds once the fee starts.</p><h2>Rest-time protection for Nevada workers</h2><p>Nevada adds a practical shield for shift workers. Under NRS 6.190 your employer cannot require you to work within 8 hours before the time you must report to court. If you serve 4 hours or more on a given day, the employer also cannot make you work that evening. It may not force you to spend sick leave or vacation either. The law expects notice from you: the summons includes a notice for your employer, and it should reach them at least 3 days before you report. In Clark County, a deferral is requested through the online jury questionnaire, with supporting documents sent afterwards by email.</p>`,
  },
  NH: {
    intro: `Work out New Hampshire juror pay of $10 a half day, see what RSA 500-A says about your job, and check the age 70 excuse and the month-long term.`,
    resume: `New Hampshire pays its jurors by the half day: RSA 500-A:15 sets $10 for each half day of attendance at the superior court, so a full day brings $20, plus 20 cents a mile round trip if you live outside the town or city where the courthouse sits. No statute we read makes a private employer keep paying wages while you serve; RSA 500-A:14 protects the job instead, and an employee fired for jury service can sue within one year for lost wages and reinstatement. Petit jurors are summoned for a month and may sit on several trials, though not beyond 30 days unless a trial is still running. Residents aged 70 or older may ask to be excused through the eResponse questionnaire. Once you have actually attended, RSA 500-A:16 keeps you off the master list for at least three years. Failing to appear and then to show cause is a misdemeanor under RSA 500-A:20, presumed class B, with a fine of up to $1,200.`,
    body: `<h2>A month on call in New Hampshire</h2><p>Unlike the one day or one trial systems of many states, a New Hampshire petit juror is summoned for a month. During that month you may be called for jury selection more than once and may sit on more than one trial, within a ceiling of 30 days, or six five-day weeks, unless a trial you are on is still going. Grand jurors usually sit one to three days a month. Plan with your employer for the whole month, not one day, and keep the attendance record: the fee is counted by the half day.</p><h2>Deferring or being excused through eResponse</h2><p>Requests go through the eResponse questionnaire, and a judge rules on each one. A deferral moves you to another month and suits a temporary obstacle: an out-of-state student term, a legislator in session, a pending case, a short-term medical condition or a hardship. A full excuse rests on RSA 500-A:11, which allows it for undue hardship, extreme inconvenience, public necessity or another cause the court finds appropriate; active military service, service in the superior court in the last three years and age 70 or older are also listed by the court.</p>`,
    days: 5,
  },
  NJ: {
    intro: `Calculate New Jersey's stepped juror fee, which jumps from $5 to $40 a day on day four, and check the one-time deferral and the long list of excuses.`,
    days: 5,
    resume: `New Jersey pays petit jurors very little at first: $5 a day for the first 3 days, then $40 a day from day 4, under N.J.S.A. 2B:20-16; grand and State grand jurors stay at $5 a day. Full-time State employees get no fee but keep their regular wages. Private employers have no statutory duty to keep paying you, though N.J.S.A. 2B:20-17 protects the job. Anyone 75 or older may ask to be excused by giving a date of birth to the county Jury Management Office, either for the coming date or permanently. If you served in the same county within the last 3 years you may ask to be excused, but it is not automatic and federal service does not count. After the qualification questionnaire, you can defer once through the My Jury Service Portal or the county office. No term length or mileage rate was read, and for a no-show the court site mentions only further court action, without a fine or jail figure.`,
    body: `<h2>The New Jersey day-four jump in pay</h2><p>The New Jersey schedule is built for long trials. A juror who is released after one or two days collects $5 per day, which barely covers a coffee and parking. From the fourth day, the rate climbs to $40, eight times higher, and stays there for the rest of the trial. Run the calculator at five days to see the change: three days at $5 plus two at $40. Grand jurors do not get the step and remain at $5 a day. State employees working full time are paid their usual salary instead of any fee.</p><h2>New Jersey excuses that need paperwork</h2><p>New Jersey asks you to finish the qualification questionnaire before anything else, then send documents to the county Jury Management Office by mail or email. The accepted grounds are unusually detailed: severe financial hardship, a medical condition unlikely to change within a year, sole care of a minor child or of a sick, aged or infirm dependent, nursing or expressing milk for a child one year or younger, being a full-time teacher with no substitute, active-duty military service, or being an active volunteer firefighter or rescue squad member. Expect to provide a certification, a doctor's form, a letter from an employer or superintendent, or orders. A one-time deferral can instead name a reason such as travel, transportation or employment.</p>`,
  },
  NM: {
    intro: `Estimate New Mexico juror pay, which runs by the hour at $12, add mileage beyond 40 miles, and check the narrow list of excuses the courts accept.`,
    resume: `New Mexico is one of the few states that pays jurors by the hour rather than by the day: $12.00 per hour, set by a New Mexico Supreme Court order under NMSA 1978 § 38-5-15. Employers owe no wages for the time, but under NMSA §§ 38-5-18 and 38-5-19 they cannot make you spend annual, vacation or sick leave, and doing so is a petty misdemeanor. People 75 or older may ask for a permanent exemption, filing one request with the district court and one with the magistrate court of their county (§ 38-5-2). Each court sets the length of service, shown on the summons. You generally cannot be required to serve in a New Mexico state court more than once every 3 years, with proof of actual service. One postponement can be requested through the online Jury Questionnaire. Willfully failing to appear is punishable under § 38-5-10 and can bring jail, a fine or both; the maximums were not stated in what we read.`,
    body: `<h2>Hourly juror pay in New Mexico</h2><p>Because New Mexico counts hours, your total depends on how long the court keeps you each day rather than on a flat daily figure. At $12.00 an hour, a full day in the courthouse is worth far more than a short morning of check-in. Travel is reimbursed separately at $0.70 a mile, round trip, but only for the miles above 40, so jurors who live close to the courthouse get no mileage. Keep your own note of arrival and release times to compare against what you are paid.</p><h2>Postponing or being excused in New Mexico</h2><p>The online Jury Questionnaire is where a New Mexico juror asks to move the date. One postponement can be requested there; a second is allowed only for an unforeseen extreme emergency. Until you are told the request was granted, you must still appear. Full excusal is granted only in very limited circumstances, in writing and with documents: age 75 or older on request, a medical condition or disability backed by a signed statement from a healthcare provider, or service within the past 3 years. The courts state that moral or religious beliefs are not a ground for excusal, so a request on that basis alone will not be accepted.</p>`,
  },
  NY: {
    intro: `Calculate New York's $72 daily juror fee, see whether your employer must cover the first three days, and learn how long you stay off the jury list afterwards.`,
    days: 5,
    resume: `New York state courts pay $72 per day of attendance under N.Y. Judiciary Law § 521 (amended in 2025). The fee works together with wages: an employer with more than 10 employees may not withhold the first $72 of your daily wages for the first 3 days of service (§ 519), and in that case you do not also receive the state fee; if your wages are below $72, the state pays the difference. The qualifications set a minimum age of 18 and no upper limit or age exemption (§ 510). Trial jurors serve no more than five court days of attendance unless a trial they sit on runs longer. Afterwards you are not eligible again for 6 years, or 8 if service lasted more than 10 days. One postponement to a definite date within 6 months is available from the county commissioner of jurors. Failing to return the questionnaire or to appear carries a civil penalty of up to $250 under § 527.`,
    body: `<h2>Who pays a New York juror on days one to three</h2><p>New York splits the cost between employers and the state. If you work for an employer with more than 10 employees, it must keep paying at least the first $72 of your daily wages for the first 3 days, and the state fee is not paid on top for those days. If your daily wages are under $72, the state makes up the gap so you still reach that figure. From day 4, an employer may stop paying, and the state's $72 daily fee is what you receive. Set the calculator to five days, the usual maximum, to see both stages.</p><h2>A long rest period after New York service</h2><p>New York offers one of the longest gaps between summonses: after serving in state court you are not eligible again for 6 years from your last day, and for 8 years if your service lasted more than 10 days. There is no age exemption, so older residents use the general grounds of § 517 instead: mental or physical incapacity, or undue hardship or extreme inconvenience to you, a person in your care or the public. A breastfeeding mother may postpone service for up to 2 years, well beyond the standard single postponement of up to 6 months.</p>`,
  },
  NC: {
    intro: `Use the calculator for North Carolina's three-step juror fee, from $12 on day one to $40 after day five, and see how the age 72 statement works.`,
    days: 6,
    resume: `North Carolina sets one uniform fee schedule under N.C. Gen. Stat. § 7A-312: $12 for the first day, $20 for each day after, and $40 a day for every day beyond 5 days served in any 24-month period; grand jurors get $20 a day. Employers are not required to keep paying wages; § 9-32 only forbids firing or demoting an employee for serving. At 72 or older you may ask to be excused, deferred or exempted with a signed statement filed at least 5 business days before the report date, without appearing (§ 9-6.1). Service lasts one week unless you are on a trial, which you must finish. A person who served in the preceding two years is not qualified, so cannot be required to serve again in that period. Excuses are granted only for compelling personal hardship or for public welfare, health or safety. Failing to appear without a sufficient excuse can bring a fine capped at $50 (§ 9-13), with no jail term.`,
    body: `<h2>The North Carolina fee steps over 24 months</h2><p>North Carolina counts long service across a rolling 24-month window rather than within a single trial. Day one pays $12, days two to five pay $20 each, and every day beyond 5 days served in that window pays $40. Someone called twice in two years can therefore reach the higher rate sooner. Grand jurors are paid $20 a day. Set the calculator to six days to see the first $40 day appear. These figures were read on an archived copy of the legislature page, since the official site was not reachable directly.</p><h2>Excuses and deferrals in North Carolina districts</h2><p>Requests to be excused or deferred are decided by the chief district court judge, or by the clerk or staff that judge designates, under procedures each district sets. Jurors 72 or older, full-time students at an out-of-state college with classes or exams, and people with a disability that interferes with service may file a signed statement without coming to court, at least 5 business days before the report date. Other requests use the form from the Administrative Office of the Courts and must show compelling personal hardship. A judge can require an excused juror to serve at a later session, and deferred jurors are placed on a later session.</p>`,
  },
  ND: {
    intro: `See what North Dakota's $100 juror fee comes to for your service, why a short first day pays $50, and how the ten-day cap protects you.`,
    resume: `North Dakota pays one of the higher state juror fees: $100 for each day of required attendance in district court, paid by the state, plus mileage at the state-employee rate (N.D. Cent. Code § 27-09.1-14). The exception is a short first day: if attendance that day lasts four hours or less, it pays $50. The jury chapter places no duty on employers to pay wages, but § 27-09.1-17 protects the job. There is no age exemption: under § 27-09.1-10 no qualified juror is exempt, and older people can only ask to be excused for undue hardship. Trial service is capped at ten court days in any two-year period, unless you are needed to finish a case, and at one grand jury in the same period. The court may excuse you for undue hardship, extreme inconvenience or public necessity for a period it chooses, after which you report again. The chapter's penalty section for failing to serve, § 27-09.1-16, was repealed in 1993, so the chapter sets no specific penalty for not appearing.`,
    body: `<h2>North Dakota's $100 day and the four-hour rule</h2><p>At $100 a day, North Dakota pays well above many states, and the state pays it rather than the county. The single adjustment concerns the first day: when you are released after four hours or less, that day is paid at $50. Every later day of required attendance pays the full $100. A juror sent home at midday on day one and then seated for a two-day trial would therefore receive $50 plus $200, with mileage added at the state-employee rate.</p><h2>Temporary excuses and the ten-day cap in North Dakota</h2><p>North Dakota treats excuses as temporary. You raise the reason on the qualification form or in an interview, and the court can excuse you for a period it considers necessary, then set a new date to report; in practice this works as a postponement rather than a release. The accepted grounds are undue hardship, extreme inconvenience and public necessity. In return, the state limits how much it can ask: no more than ten court days as a trial juror and one grand jury in any two-year period, unless a case in progress needs you longer. No separate waiting period between summonses is set.</p>`,
  },
  OH: {
    intro: `Check how your Ohio county sets juror pay, when it must rise after ten days, and how the automatic postponement and escalating contempt penalties work.`,
    resume: `Ohio leaves the daily juror rate to each board of county commissioners, so there is no statewide figure, but Ohio Rev. Code § 2313.22 forces a raise for long service: after 10 days of actual service, pay becomes the greater of $15 or 1.5 times the county rate, and a county may go up to 2 times. Employers owe no wages during service, and § 2313.19 bars them from requiring or requesting use of vacation, sick or annual leave; give reasonable notice of the summons before service starts. A juror over 75 is excused on request, by telling the court's jury employee before the reporting date. Once you have served two consecutive calendar weeks you are discharged unless a trial continues, and you are not called again until the second jury year after that service. One postponement is automatic if asked at least two business days ahead. Not attending is contempt under §§ 2313.20 and 2313.99(A), from $250 and 30 days for a first offense upward.`,
    body: `<h2>Ohio's automatic postponement and small employer rule</h2><p>Ohio makes moving your date simple. Ask by phone, in writing or by email at least two business days before your first appearance and agree on a new date within six months, and the first postponement is granted automatically. Later ones are only for extreme emergencies. Small businesses get extra relief: if your employer has 25 or fewer staff and another employee served in the prior 30 days, you receive an automatic postponement. Excuses for hardship need documentation, and the courts state that missing work alone is not a hardship. An excuse lasts only for that jury year unless its grounds are permanent.</p><h2>Escalating penalties for missing Ohio jury duty</h2><p>Ohio is unusually specific about repeat no-shows. A first failure to attend is punishable as contempt by up to $250 and 30 days in jail, a second by up to $500 and 60 days, and a third or later by up to $1,000 and 90 days. The list of lawful excuses is broad, including a recent death or dangerous illness of a spouse or near relative, caregiving with no substitute, breastfeeding a baby one year old or younger, active military duty, membership of a cloistered religious order or an active Amish sect, and age over 75 on request.</p>`,
  },
  OK: {
    intro: `Estimate Oklahoma's $50 daily juror pay and the Lengthy Trial Fund top-up, and see why one day per calendar year is usually all you serve.`,
    resume: `Oklahoma jurors receive $50 per day of attendance plus daily round-trip mileage under the State Travel Reimbursement Act (28 Okla. Stat. § 86). On trials longer than 10 days, the Lengthy Trial Fund can add wage replacement of up to $200 a day from day 11, and up to $50 a day for days 4 to 10 on proven hardship. Employers need not pay wages unless you choose to use paid leave, and they cannot require or request that you do; firing or other adverse action is a misdemeanor with a fine of up to $5,000 under 38 Okla. Stat. § 34. Over 70, you cannot be compelled to serve. Service is one day per calendar year unless you are selected for a trial, and anyone who served in the five preceding calendar years cannot be compelled again. One postponement is available as of right to a date within six months. We found no juror-specific penalty for not appearing in Title 38, and the statutes database was unreachable.`,
    body: `<h2>The Oklahoma Lengthy Trial Fund</h2><p>For most jurors, Oklahoma pays a flat $50 per day. The picture changes on long cases. When a trial runs beyond 10 days, the Lengthy Trial Fund may replace lost wages at up to $200 a day starting on day 11. Jurors who can prove hardship may also receive up to $50 a day extra for days 4 through 10. This matters for self-employed people and hourly workers who would otherwise lose income, since employers are not obliged to keep paying. Mileage is paid daily for the round trip, and some courts provide parking.</p><h2>Postponements tied to small Oklahoma employers</h2><p>Oklahoma grants one postponement as of right: contact the court clerk by phone, by email, in writing or in person and set a new date within six months. A second requires an extreme emergency. A further rule protects tiny workplaces: if your employer has 5 or fewer full-time staff and a co-worker was summoned for the same period, your service is postponed automatically. For an excuse, ask the judge before the reporting date with documents such as tax returns, medical statements or proof of dependency. Missing work alone does not count as hardship, and an excuse that is not permanent lasts two years.</p>`,
  },
  OR: {
    intro: `Work out Oregon circuit court pay, $10 then $25 a day, plus mileage or transit tickets, and check the excuse rules for people 70 and older or breastfeeding.`,
    days: 5,
    resume: `Oregon circuit courts pay jurors $10 a day for the first two days and $25 a day from day 3 (ORS 10.061, 10.065), with mileage at 20 cents per mile round trip. If your employer keeps paying you, you must waive the fee unless your employment agreement says otherwise. No Oregon law requires wages during service, but employers must allow unpaid leave, cannot force use of paid leave, and with 10 or more employees must keep your insurance if you elect it (ORS 10.090, 10.092). Anyone 70 or older is excused on request but must still respond to the summons (ORS 10.050(3)). The statutory goal for circuit courts is one day or one trial, and no trial juror serves more than 10 days. Service in an Oregon state or federal court within the last 24 months makes you ineligible. A no-show can be held in contempt: a fine of up to $500 or 1% of annual gross income, whichever is greater, and up to six months in jail.`,
    body: `<h2>Oregon fee waivers and insurance during service</h2><p>Oregon links the juror fee to your paycheck. Jurors whose employer continues their pay must give up the $10 or $25 daily fee, unless their employment agreement provides otherwise. For everyone else, the fee steps up on day 3, so a five-day trial pays two days at $10 and three at $25. Employers with 10 or more employees must keep your insurance coverage in place while you serve if you elect it, provided you notify them under their notification policy. Public transit riders can claim their tickets instead of mileage.</p><h2>Deferring and excusing jury service in Oregon</h2><p>In Oregon, you complete the qualification form in the county eResponse portal, then request a postponement there (phone and mail also work). One deferral is granted for good cause to a term starting within a year; a further one needs good cause and ten available dates in the following six months. Grounds for excusal include age 70 or older, undue hardship or extreme inconvenience to you, your family, your employer or the public, breastfeeding, and being the sole caregiver of a child or dependent who cannot afford other care. Members of the active Oregon National Guard may treat service as optional. The law requires no permanent excusal.</p>`,
  },
  PA: {
    intro: `Calculate Pennsylvania juror pay, $9 for three days then $25, check whether your employer is covered by job protection, and review the statutory excuses.`,
    days: 5,
    resume: `Under 42 Pa.C.S. § 4561, Pennsylvania pays $9 a day for the first three days of jury service in a calendar year and $25 for each later day that year, with mileage at 17 cents per mile round trip. Employers have no duty to pay wages, and firing or penalizing a juror is a summary offense (§ 4563), but that protection excludes retail or service employers with fewer than 15 staff and manufacturers with fewer than 40; those employees may ask to be excused. At 75 or older you can be excused on request (§ 4503(a)(6)). Each county sets the term, and many, such as Lehigh, use one day or one trial. After serving you are exempt for 3 years, or only 1 year if you served fewer than 3 days that year. Only the statutory grounds count as excuses, and the county jury office decides hardship. Failing to appear is contempt under § 4584: a fine of up to $500, up to 10 days in jail, or both.`,
    body: `<h2>Pennsylvania counts juror days per calendar year</h2><p>The $9 rate in Pennsylvania covers your first three days of service in a calendar year, not per trial. A juror who already served two days in March and is called again in October moves to $25 sooner. Mileage is paid at 17 cents per mile for the round trip everywhere except Philadelphia, the first judicial district, where none is paid. The calculator at five days shows three days at $9 and two at $25.</p><h2>Small employers and other Pennsylvania excuse grounds</h2><p>Pennsylvania job protection has a size threshold. Retail and service businesses with fewer than 15 employees and manufacturers with fewer than 40 are outside it, and their employees may ask to be excused instead. Other statutory grounds include active military service, undue hardship or extreme inconvenience, jury service within the last 3 years, being a close relative of a criminal homicide victim, having served on an 18-month statewide investigating grand jury, sitting as a judge, breastfeeding on request, and age 75 or older on request. Requests go to the county jury office. In Lehigh County, for example, one deferment can be chosen in the online questionnaire.</p>`,
  },
  RI: {
    intro: `Estimate Rhode Island's $25 daily Superior Court fee, see the one day or two day service pattern by county, and learn how to defer through eResponse.`,
    resume: `Rhode Island pays $25 for each day of attendance at Superior Court, a rate in force since July 1, 2022, under R.I. Gen. Laws § 9-29-5; no fee is paid for weekends, holidays or days you are excused. An employer has no duty to pay wages unless a contract or collective agreement requires it, but under § 9-9-28 it may not take away your job, raises, promotions or benefits, and a violation is a misdemeanor. Service is short: two days or one trial in Providence and Kent counties, and usually one day or one trial in Washington and Newport counties. Nobody serves more than once every three years. The statutory age rule and excuse grounds in § 9-9-3 and § 9-10-9 could not be read, as the legislature site was unreachable on October 29, 2026. A juror who fails to attend without a satisfactory excuse can be held in contempt and fined at least $20 (§ 9-10-10).`,
    body: `<h2>Short service terms across Rhode Island counties</h2><p>Rhode Island keeps the commitment brief, though it differs by county. In Providence and Kent counties, the standard is two days or one trial, whichever applies. In Washington and Newport counties, it is usually one day or one trial. The $25 fee is paid only for days you actually attend, so weekends, holidays and days you are excused earn nothing. In Providence County, free parking and a shuttle reduce the cost of getting there, and no mileage rate was set in what we read.</p><h2>Deferring through Rhode Island's eResponse</h2><p>Once your summons arrives, log in to Jury Service eResponse and choose the option to apply to defer, then pick a new date on the calendar; the Jury Commissioner approves or denies it. Requests to be excused or disqualified are filed through the same system and decided by the Office of the Jury Commissioner, which asks people to seek an excuse only for urgent reasons. Because the statutory list of grounds could not be read, we cannot confirm which reasons Rhode Island accepts, so describe your situation fully in eResponse and keep any supporting documents ready.</p>`,
  },
  SC: {
    intro: `Check South Carolina juror pay set county by county, the exemption for people 65 and older, and the affidavit rules for caregivers and essential workers.`,
    resume: `South Carolina sets juror pay county by county. S.C. Code § 14-7-1370 still lists old per diems of $2 to $10 depending on the county, but counties now pay more (Georgetown County, for example, pays $25 a day), so any figure shown is a minimum. Pay arrives whether or not you sit on a case, and mileage varies by county from a 5-cent statutory base. No state law requires wages during service, but an employer who fires or demotes you for it can be sued for up to one year's wages (§ 41-1-70). At 65 or older you are exempt on request, and a phone call to the clerk of court confirming your date of birth is enough (§ 14-7-840). Circuit court jurors are summoned for a term beginning in a given week. You are not drawn more than once every three calendar years. Students and school employees can move service outside the school term. Missing service without a sufficient excuse carries a civil penalty of up to $100 under § 14-7-1390.`,
    body: `<h2>Caregiver and business affidavits in South Carolina</h2><p>South Carolina recognizes several grounds that need a notarized affidavit filed with the clerk of court: legal custody of a child under 7 without adequate childcare, being the primary caretaker of a person 65 or older or a severely disabled person, and being essential to a business that would have to close. A mental or physical condition calls for a doctor's statement. You mark the ground on the summons response form or in the Juror Web Portal. Misusing these excuse rules is itself a misdemeanor under § 14-7-860(E), punishable by up to $1,000 and/or 30 days.</p><h2>South Carolina school term and work transfers</h2><p>Rather than excusing students and school employees, South Carolina moves their service outside the school term on request, with proof. Essential workers can be transferred to another term by affidavit. The age exemption is among the earliest anywhere, starting at 65, and needs nothing more than a call to the clerk. On frequency, the statute says no one is drawn more than once every three calendar years and never more than once a year, while the court's web page cites two years. County pay rates vary widely, so check the figure your county states.</p>`,
  },
  SD: {
    intro: `Estimate South Dakota juror pay, $50 when seated or a $10 appearance fee, plus mileage, and check the breastfeeding, new-parent and age 80 excuses.`,
    resume: `South Dakota pays $50 for each day you sit on a case, but only a $10 appearance fee if you are called and not seated, under SDCL 16-13-46; the county pays, and mileage follows the state rate for each mile actually traveled. The law keeps your job status, pay rate and seniority, but paying wages during the absence is up to the employer, and firing or suspending a juror is a Class 2 misdemeanor (SDCL 16-13-41.1, 16-13-41.2). People 80 or older may ask to be excused; the judge gives that substantial weight but can refuse. Each circuit's presiding judge decides how panels are used, and your term appears in the UJS Juror Portal. If you have already served since the current master list was drawn, you are skipped; the list is compiled each year. Unavailable dates can be entered in the online questionnaire. Contempt brings a fine of $50 to $500, and ignoring a second order of attachment can mean up to 10 days in county jail.`,
    body: `<h2>Seated versus called in South Dakota</h2><p>Pay in South Dakota depends on whether you end up on a jury. If you report but are not seated, the county pays a $10 appearance fee. Once you sit on a case, each day pays $50. Mileage is added for the miles you actually travel, and the court system lists $0.76 per mile round trip. If you were only called for selection, count those days at $10 rather than $50.</p><h2>New-parent and breastfeeding requests in South Dakota</h2><p>South Dakota names specific family situations. You may ask to be excused if you are breastfeeding a baby under one year, or if you are the parent of a baby under six weeks old or one expected during your service. These requests must be made in writing within 10 days of receiving the summons. Clergy whose religious beliefs conflict with service may also ask, as may people 80 or older, and the presiding judge can approve other reasons. Send a written request to the clerk of courts; the presiding judge decides. Students should contact the clerk directly, and written proof may be requested.</p>`,
  },
  TN: {
    intro: `See how Tennessee county juror pay combines with the rule that employers with five or more workers keep paying wages, and check the age 75 declaration.`,
    days: 10,
    resume: `Tennessee lets each county set juror pay under Tenn. Code Ann. § 22-4-101, and the statewide minimum could not be read on an official site; county examples are Blount at $10 a day, Knox at $11 and Hamilton at $25. The employer rule is what matters most: under § 22-4-106, employers with 5 or more regular employees must pay your usual wages for time spent serving and traveling, minus the juror fee. It does not cover employers with fewer than 5 regular employees or temporary workers employed under 6 months. Show the summons to your immediate superior on the next workday after you receive it. There is no automatic age exemption: at 75 or older you are excused by filing a written declaration that a mental or physical condition prevents service (§ 22-1-103). Terms are set by county, for example 2 weeks in Knox and 2 or 3 weeks in Hamilton. A statewide penalty maximum was not found; Knox County says a no-show may be held in contempt and fined $500.`,
    body: `<h2>Tennessee wages minus the juror fee</h2><p>Tennessee is one of the states where many workers keep their paycheck during jury service. If your employer has 5 or more regular employees, it must pay your usual wages for the time you spend serving and traveling to court, and may deduct the county juror fee. The duty does not reach businesses with fewer than 5 regular employees, nor temporary workers employed for less than 6 months. Because terms can run 2 weeks or more, the calculator opens at ten days. Notify your immediate superior by showing the summons on your next workday.</p><h2>Postponing by county in Tennessee</h2><p>Tennessee leaves postponement to county jury coordinators or clerks. In Knox County, ask at orientation or contact the Jury Coordinator. In Hamilton County, make the request at least 10 days ahead and choose a new 2- or 3-week term. For an excuse, send the county jury clerk a jury service affidavit with documentation before your date. Recognized grounds include undue or extreme physical or financial hardship, a mental or physical condition, caregiving without a substitute, costs that harm necessary daily living expenses, out-of-state employment without compensation, and age 75 or older with a disabling condition.</p>`,
  },
  TX: {
    intro: `Calculate Texas juror pay from the state minimums of $20 and $58, check the age 75 and child-care exemptions, and see how the automatic postponement works.`,
    resume: `Texas sets only minimums for juror pay: at least $20 for the first day and at least $58 for each later day, under Tex. Gov't Code § 61.001, with each county's commissioners court free to pay more. No Texas statute requires private employers to pay wages during service, though Tex. Civ. Prac. & Rem. Code §§ 122.001-122.0022 protect the job, and to be reinstated after an unlawful firing you must tell the employer as soon as practical after release that you intend to return. People 75 or older may claim a temporary or permanent exemption by filing a signed statement with the clerk (§§ 62.106, 62.108). The first postponement is automatic, if you had none in the past year, to a date within 6 months. We did not read a term length or a one-day-one-trial rule. Ignoring a summons or giving false exemption information can bring a contempt fine of $100 to $1,000 (§ 62.0141).`,
    body: `<h2>Texas exemptions you claim by signed statement</h2><p>Texas lets several groups opt out by filing a signed statement with the clerk before the reporting date: people 75 or older, anyone with legal custody of a child under 12 who would be left without adequate supervision, secondary school students, college or university students in attendance, legislative branch officers or employees, primary caretakers of a person unable to care for themselves, and active-duty military deployed away from home station. A religious holy day and a physical or mental impairment are also listed. The judge may accept any other reasonable sworn excuse, but an economic excuse needs the approval of the parties.</p><h2>Postponing a Texas summons</h2><p>Texas makes the first postponement close to automatic. Contact the court clerk in person, in writing or by phone before your date; if you have not postponed in the past year, it is granted to a date within 6 months. Later postponements require an extreme emergency. Pay depends on the county, so the $20 and $58 amounts are floors. Separately from contempt, § 62.111 sets a fine of $100 to $500 for a juror who fails to attend without reasonable excuse.</p>`,
  },
  UT: {
    intro: `Estimate Utah juror pay, $18.50 then $49 a day, add the long-distance mileage, and see how the one day or one trial rule limits your time.`,
    resume: `Utah fixes juror pay in state law: $18.50 for the first day and $49 for each later day under Utah Code § 78B-1-119, with mileage of $1 for each 4 miles beyond 50 miles one way; the court FAQ adds that parking or transit costs may be reimbursed with proof of payment. Employers need not pay wages, but § 78B-1-116 forbids making you use annual, vacation or sick leave. Utah has no age exemption, since § 78B-1-108 says no qualified prospective juror is exempt; an older person can still ask to be excused for undue hardship or incapacity. Service follows one day or one trial, within a qualification term of 1 month in Salt Lake County district courts, 3 months in Davis, Utah and Weber, and 6 months elsewhere. After attending court you cannot be summoned again for at least two years. The court may postpone service for good cause. Failing to appear or to complete service is contempt: up to a $1,000 fine, 30 days in jail, or both (§§ 78B-1-115, 78B-6-310).`,
    body: `<h2>Utah qualification terms by county</h2><p>Under one day or one trial, a Utah juror who is not selected is done after a single day, and a juror who is selected serves for that trial. What varies is the window during which you can be called: 1 month for Salt Lake County district courts, 3 months for Davis, Utah and Weber counties, and 6 months in the rest of the state. Pay starts at $18.50 for day one and rises to $49 from day two, and mileage applies only to the distance beyond 50 miles one way, at $1 per 4 miles.</p><h2>Excuses backed by evidence in Utah</h2><p>Utah asks for proof. To be excused, support your request with an affidavit, sworn testimony or other evidence. The grounds in § 78B-1-109 are undue hardship, public necessity, being a breastfeeding mother, or being incapable of jury service, and an excuse lasts as long as its reason exists. To postpone, show good cause and contact the court using the number on your notice. Before a justice court judge or commissioner, the contempt maximum is lower, $500 and 5 days.</p>`,
  },
  VT: {
    intro: `See what Vermont pays jurors on request, $30 a day, how its two-year term caps your trips to court, and why no one is excused automatically.`,
    resume: `Vermont Superior Court jurors receive $30 a day on request under 32 V.S.A. § 1511, unless their employer already paid them for the day, and mileage at the state-employee rate is paid only on request with a showing of hardship. Employers have no duty to pay wages, but 21 V.S.A. § 499 lets you keep seniority, fringe benefits and vacation credit while serving. There is no age exemption: under 4 V.S.A. § 962 nobody is excused automatically, and the judge may excuse a person who asks and shows undue hardship. Service runs on a two-year term, during which you can be called to jury selection at most 3 times and to a trial date at most 3 times, and your name can go on a state court venire only once in any two-year period. An individual hardship request can excuse you for part of the term. Not appearing without a satisfactory excuse brings a civil penalty of up to $200, which can be waived for good cause (4 V.S.A. §§ 958, 961).`,
    body: `<h2>Vermont's two-year term with built-in limits</h2><p>Instead of a single date, a Vermont summons places you on a two-year term. During that period, the court can call you to jury selection no more than 3 times and to a trial date no more than 3 times. Your name may be placed on a venire for a state court only once in any two-year period. The $30 daily fee must be requested and is not paid if your employer already paid you; mileage is paid only on request and on a showing of hardship.</p><h2>Asking a Vermont judge for an excuse</h2><p>Vermont grants no automatic exemptions, by age or by occupation. You make an individual request to the presiding judge and show undue hardship, either on you or on your employer. A judge may excuse you for part of the two-year term, which in practice works as a postponement. People who are not physically or mentally able to serve are not qualified at all. The court's own procedure for these requests could not be read when we checked. Ignoring an order to return the questionnaire can be treated as contempt.</p>`,
  },
  VA: {
    intro: `Calculate Virginia's $50 daily juror allowance, check the evening shift protection for workers, and review the many exemptions available on request.`,
    resume: `Virginia pays jurors $50 per day of attendance under Va. Code § 17.1-618, an amount meant to cover travel and other expenses, with no separate mileage rate. Employers owe no wages, but § 18.2-465.1 bars them from forcing you to use sick leave or vacation, and after 4 or more hours of jury duty you cannot be required to start a shift beginning at or after 5 p.m. that day or before 3 a.m. the next day. Anyone over 73 may ask to be exempted (§ 8.01-341.1(10)). Service covers one term of court, which can last up to four months depending on the circuit. If you reported for jury duty in a state court in the previous three years, you are left off the next jury list. Call the number on the summons to request a deferral for genuine hardship. A juror who fails to appear without sufficient excuse is fined $50 to $200 under § 8.01-356, and the court may also treat it as contempt.`,
    body: `<h2>Virginia limits on late shifts after jury duty</h2><p>Virginia protects workers from being sent straight from the courthouse to a night shift. Once you have spent 4 hours or more on jury duty in a day, your employer cannot require you to start a shift that begins at or after 5 p.m. that day, or one that begins before 3 a.m. the following day. Employers also cannot make you use sick leave or vacation for service. The $50 daily allowance is the only payment, intended to cover travel and other costs.</p><h2>Exemptions on request and deferrals in Virginia</h2><p>Virginia lists many people who may serve but are exempt if they ask: mariners in maritime service, a parent with custody of a child 16 or younger needing continuous care or of a child under 18 with a serious health condition, a breastfeeding mother, a caregiver of a person with a serious health condition, anyone over 73, a spouse summoned to the same panel, the sole essential worker of a business or farm, and others. An occupational inconvenience, including for full-time students and parents of children 16 or younger, can move service to the next term. Since a term can last up to four months, a deferral to the next one is often the practical choice.</p>`,
  },
  WA: {
    intro: `Check your Washington county's juror fee, set between $10 and $25, the excuse for people 80 and older, and the coming breastfeeding exemption.`,
    resume: `Washington lets each county set a uniform daily juror fee between $10 and $25 under RCW 2.36.150, and most counties pay $10, so treat $10 as the common floor; mileage at the state rate is paid on top for each day of attendance. Employers must grant leave for jury service but are not required to pay wages (RCW 2.36.165). People 80 or older who attest they cannot serve for health reasons must be excused on request, with no doctor's note required (RCW 2.36.100(2)). Each court sets its own terms; state policy says a term should ideally be one week or less, and service one day or one trial, whichever is longer. If you are summoned again within 12 months after serving at least one week in that period, you may ask in writing to be excused. To reschedule, call or write to the contact on the summons. Intentionally failing to appear is a misdemeanor, punishable by up to 90 days in jail and/or a $1,000 fine (RCW 2.36.170 and RCW 9.92.030).`,
    body: `<h2>A misdemeanor for skipping Washington jury duty</h2><p>Washington treats an intentional failure to appear more seriously than many states. Rather than a civil fine, it is a misdemeanor under RCW 2.36.170, which carries the general misdemeanor penalty of up to 90 days in jail and/or a $1,000 fine. That makes responding to the summons, or asking for a new date, the only sensible course. The court named on the summons can reassign an excused juror to another term within 12 months, so rescheduling is generally available.</p><h2>Washington grounds for excusal, including a 2027 change</h2><p>Washington courts may excuse a juror for undue hardship, extreme inconvenience, public necessity, illness or any reason the court deems sufficient. People 80 or older who attest that health prevents them from serving must be excused without a doctor's note. A new breastfeeding exemption, for a parent of an infant under 24 months, is added to RCW 2.36.100 from January 1, 2027. Before then, a breastfeeding parent can still raise hardship or inconvenience. Ask the court on the summons, and note that the fee varies by county within the $10 to $25 band.</p>`,
  },
  WV: {
    intro: `Estimate West Virginia juror pay, up to $40 a day plus mileage from the State Treasury, and check the age 70 excuse and the written postponement request.`,
    resume: `West Virginia's statute, W. Va. Code § 52-1-17, lets each circuit court set the daily juror reimbursement between $15 and $40, and the state court system says jurors receive $40 a day plus round-trip mileage, paid by the State Treasury. Employers must excuse you for the days of service but need not pay wages (§§ 52-1-21, 52-3-1); show the summons to your immediate supervisor on the next working day after receiving it. People 70 or older are qualified but must be excused if they ask, using a box on the qualification questionnaire (§ 52-1-8(c)). Service is for a term of court, and within any two years no one attends more than 30 court days, unless a case needs it, or serves as a petit juror at more than one term. Having been paid as a juror in the previous two years disqualifies you. To postpone, write to the presiding circuit judge. A no-show without good cause is civil contempt with a fine of up to $1,000 (§ 52-1-24).`,
    body: `<h2>West Virginia's call-in system and 30-day cap</h2><p>A West Virginia summons places you on a term of court, but a call-in system means you are not expected in person every day of it. Across any two years, the law caps attendance at 30 court days, except to finish a case, and allows service as a petit juror at only one term. Anyone who attended and was paid as a petit, grand or magistrate juror in the previous two years is disqualified. The fee range runs from $15 to $40 by statute, with the state court system citing $40.</p><h2>Written requests to a West Virginia judge</h2><p>West Virginia exempts no occupation automatically. Requests to be excused go in writing to the presiding circuit judge, who may grant a temporary excuse, for a set period, on grounds of undue hardship, extreme inconvenience or public necessity; once it ends, you report as directed. Current members of the National Guard or reserves can ask to be excused, as can anyone 70 or older. A substantial physical or mental disability disqualifies a person, and a physician's certificate may be required. Your employer must release you but may only reduce pay for time you were actually away.</p>`,
  },
  WI: {
    intro: `Check Wisconsin's $16 minimum juror fee and your county's rate, see why age is never a ground on its own, and learn how deferrals replace most excuses.`,
    resume: `Wisconsin sets a statutory minimum of $16 per day for jurors under Wis. Stat. § 756.25, and each county board fixes the actual rate. Mileage at the state rate is paid for each mile traveled daily, round trip. Employers must grant leave with no loss of seniority or pay advancement, but § 756.255 does not require wages. Wisconsin abolished exemptions and bars excluding a qualified juror because of age (§ 756.001(3)), so older residents rely on an individual excuse or deferral. Circuits may adopt one day or one trial; elsewhere, availability is capped at 31 consecutive days and at most 5 days in court unless a trial needs more. Service protects you for 4 years in counties without one day or one trial, and for 2 to 4 years, set by the judges, in circuits that use it. Failing to attend without being excused is contempt, and after a show-cause hearing the court may impose up to $500 (§ 756.30).`,
    body: `<h2>No exemptions in Wisconsin, only deferrals</h2><p>Wisconsin did away with categories of exemption. No one is excused because of age, job or status alone. Under § 756.03, the court may excuse a person who cannot fulfill a juror's responsibilities, and undue hardship or extreme inconvenience normally leads to a deferral instead of a release. Ask the court named on the summons, or the clerk of circuit court if authorized; it may move your service to a later date it sets, and documentation may be required. In practice, most hardship reasons lead to a later date rather than a release.</p><h2>How Wisconsin counties set pay and terms</h2><p>The $16 figure is only the floor. County boards set the real rate, can pay by the half-day, and in one day or one trial circuits may treat the first day differently. The term also depends on the circuit: either one day or one trial, or a window of up to 31 consecutive days with no more than 5 days in court unless a trial runs longer. The protected period after service is 4 years where one day or one trial is not used.</p>`,
  },
  WY: {
    intro: `Calculate Wyoming juror pay of $30 a day and the possible $50 rate after five days, and check which hardships do and do not count there.`,
    days: 6,
    resume: `Wyoming pays jurors $30 for each full or part day, and after more than 5 consecutive days of service (weekends and holidays not counted) the court may add $20 a day, for $50 in total (Wyo. Stat. §§ 1-11-302, 1-11-303). Mileage is paid when the one-way trip from home to court exceeds 5 miles, at the state travel rate. The jury statutes protect the job (§ 1-11-401) but contain no rule requiring wages, and an employer who punishes a juror owes up to $1,000 in exemplary damages per violation. Anyone 72 or older may ask to be excused; it is not automatic (§ 1-11-104(a)). Each court sets its own term, such as six months in Teton County. After serving, you are excused on request for the rest of that term and calendar year, and possibly the next term. No statewide postponement rule was found in the statute. A juror who willfully skips service without reasonable excuse can be arrested, brought to court and held in contempt under § 1-11-115, which sets no fine amount.`,
    body: `<h2>The optional Wyoming raise after five days</h2><p>Wyoming's base rate is $30 for each full or part day, so even a short morning counts as a paid day. On longer cases, once service passes 5 consecutive days, not counting weekends and holidays, the court may add $20, bringing the daily amount to $50. The raise is at the court's discretion rather than automatic. The calculator opens at six days to show where the higher rate could begin. Mileage starts only when your one-way trip is longer than 5 miles.</p><h2>Wyoming excuses: what counts and what does not</h2><p>Wyoming spells out that hardship or business inconvenience alone is not enough to be excused. The accepted reasons are threatened material injury or destruction of property, your own health, sickness or death of a family member, care of young children, age 72 or older on request, and having already served during the jury term. Some people are exempt on a sworn declaration, including salaried firefighters, police, elected officials, and active-duty National Guard and military members where federal law exempts them. Courts handle postponements themselves; Teton County, for instance, lets jurors ask ahead to be excused from trial dates in certain periods.</p>`,
  },
};
